semantic_diff: >-
  Base: revision 14, digest sha256:de9eb66d16967fa6a6adbf625d9c78117464cd236cfac9b95b1afc4e498a8c41,
  verified intact with pave-init 2.6.2 record_revision.py (18 entries). Apply head:
  revision 15 at its proposal's digest_after
  sha256:1331f29abc50497648035a21e717118b190c045cc64bc2bdb7fa40f5d40a8d24 - the chain
  order is v15 then v16. Drafted to
  apply after revision 15 (DECISIONS 1088 approved its two items): git apply of
  v15-graph.patch then this patch on a scratch copy validates at 21 nodes, 67 edges, 5
  control nodes after v15, and no hunk here touches a line revision 15 touches
  (two-line context for that reason; revision 15's context covers the roles hunk, so
  the order is v15 then v16). Counts unchanged by this patch (21 nodes, 67 edges, 67
  outcomes, 24 evidence definitions, 5 control nodes after v15); every check, node
  body, outcome, edge and state field equal except the two
  prose sites named below. Layer test (pave-spec 2.1, revision rule 9): a runtime
  control on what the lead may write, and the seat lifecycle at dispatch - run setup,
  no graph meaning moves. Line balance: 3 hunks, 20 lines added against 8 removed;
  2430 lines become 2442 and 138,272 bytes become 139,140 on revision 14 (2387 become
  2399 after v15). Of the 20 added, 11 are
  write_limits structure (two deny entries, five required fields each); prose is 9
  added against 7 removed. Source: the user's second answer at DECISIONS 1086 - "Yes,
  draft v16 now (Recommended)" - to the companion the revision 15 proposal named
  (lesson L5's mechanical half and lesson L8 in the lessons file
  artifacts/run/closure/lessons-run-20260827-20260920T171728Z.md; usage record item 7).

  (1) WRITE LIMITS - two deny globs bound to the lead: the workflow's own package
  repository and the user's memory directory, each with its reason and remedy.
  Evidence: DECISIONS 12 (2026-09-05, "approve stop asking for my approval") to
  DECISIONS 728 (2026-09-08, revoked) - two plugin releases into the package
  repository without approval, reverted; memory files that widened the lead's own
  authority; 3.5 days. The write_limits block stood empty through it, so the boundary
  lived only in prose and memory - the forms that failed. Enforcement level
  (lead-hooks.md, pave-spec 9.14.1): blocking, because the harm recurred inside one
  run, cost a reverted release, is irreversible before the next gate (a pushed release
  is public, an edited memory line steers every later session), and is detected by an
  exact absolute glob against the written path; the remedy is always available and is
  printed at the refusal. Bound to the lead alone - a seat's match is advice - so no
  glob strands a seat. Why these two surfaces and not "everything outside artifacts":
  a glob cannot say except, and the router's deny mode matches every Bash path token,
  reads included (hooks/pre_tool_use_router.py line 140 iterates target_paths; the
  write-only helper write_target_paths exists and serves the hook-records path at line
  181). A glob over the plugin cache or the harness directory would refuse the lead's
  own "python3 .../scripts/record_revision.py" and its skill reads and strand the run.
  The two chosen surfaces are ones the lead reads through the Read tool (not hooked)
  and never executes from. Release-layer finding for the reviewer: mode_write_limits
  should iterate write_target_paths for Bash; once it does, the plugin cache and the
  harness settings become deniable - their evidence is thinner (the installs were
  approved), so they wait for that fix and their own incident. Why absolute paths: the
  guard expands "~" in Bash tokens (bash_path_tokens) but not in globs
  (_absolute_glob_forms), so a home-relative glob is a second release-layer
  follow-up; until then the absolute path is run setup for this installation, as a
  seat's model id is. Match semantics checked against the guard's own functions: the
  package repository's VERSION file and memory/MEMORY.md match; the installed skill
  under the plugin cache, the harness settings file, run-state.json and the fork do
  not. "/Users/jinhun/GitHub/pave/*" matches file paths under the repository (the
  release writes); a bare directory token (git -C, cd) is not matched - pushes are the
  push guard's already, but git apply, checkout, merge, cherry-pick and stash pop write
  files under a directory-only token and escape this deny, a third release-layer
  follow-up (the router should resolve the files such a command writes). Shape: the
  guard's fallback parser (used when PyYAML is absent) accepts only deeper-indented
  items with single-line scalars and otherwise fails open with no limits, so the block
  is written in that shape; both parsers read it identically (checked). Cost per run:
  none - a refusal prints at the write. Caps: none. The layer allows them, but the
  evidence names no undeclared file-name group that regrew by file count; the run's
  growth was one appended log and run-state rows, which a per-checkpoint creation cap
  does not measure, and revision 15's lead-record principle cuts them. User-only: yes
  - a runtime control on what an actor may write (rule 9).

  (2) SEAT LIFECYCLE - stated once at roles.lead, the actor who dispatches, in place of
  the scheduling restatement there (scheduling_holds_recorded states that rule, and the
  lead skill row 3 repeats it). Three clauses, each with its reason: a doer seat
  retires only on the review outcome that passes its work (LEAD-LOG 2106 - the
  prepare_pr seat stopped before its review, and the repair round had no context);
  a reviewer gets one frozen stamp after every constraint has reached the producer,
  any later change being the next revision, never a re-stamp (LEAD-LOG 1707 to 1710 -
  constraints sent after the relay, a re-stamp under the same revision, the review
  round void); a record another seat is writing is read after its mtime settles
  (LEAD-LOG 2176 to 2177 - a declared file read mid-write). Each cost an hour or more
  and the run learned them as memory, not as text a fresh lead loads. Why the lead role
  and not per node: revision 4 declared seat continuity per node because it differed
  per node; these three hold for every dispatch, so one sentence at the dispatcher and
  no copy. Cut with it: realize_increment's second copy of the retained-thread rule -
  scope_next_increment's activity already names realize_increment as its sibling, so
  two copies of one rule become one. Cost per run: none; it removes a re-review round
  and a re-dispatch per incident. User-only: seat lifecycle is a seat change (run
  setup), which the lead's brief lists among user-only items - declared pending with
  the write limits.

  NO CHANGE: model or effort per seat - the graph declares none (dispatch tiering lives
  in the lead skill), so nothing to cut or add here.

  EVERY COPY (release layer, none edited here): skills/vllm-neuron-parity/SKILL.md
  "Roles and dispatch" (line 86; gains the three lifecycle clauses once) and row 6 (the
  retained thread, unchanged); README.md line 597 (dispatch mechanics);
  agents/implementer.md lines 368 to 370 ("retired when that instance closes; a repair
  round continues the seat" becomes "retired on the passing review outcome"); the
  "retired when that node instance closes" line in adversarial-reviewer.md 207,
  measurer.md 166, adjudicator.md 109, investigator.md 160, rederiver.md 136 stays -
  those seats are not doers under review; README.md line 628 and
  references/artifact-layout.md line 435 (the deny row, unchanged in substance);
  hooks/pre_tool_use_router.py line 140 (the read-matching finding above);
  tests/test_write_limits.py (its fixture is the shape used here).
